  "teaser": "Two voices in the same room, listening to each other — from Philadelphia in 1975 to Kashif and Meli'sa Morgan in 1987, an hour of soul as conversation.",
  "description": "Tonight is about singing with someone, not just beside them. We start with the songwriters behind a classic performing their own words — a different kind of intimacy — then follow the duet through Philadelphia, through Norman Connors pairing voices like a matchmaker, and into the quiet storm era where the conversations got slower and more honest.\n\nConnors keeps returning here: first with Michael Henderson and Jean Carn floating over keyboards, then shaping Al Johnson five years later. Jean Carn appears twice, and so does Teddy Pendergrass — once behind Harold Melvin, once as an equal beside Stephanie Mills. The same voices, different conditions, different years.\n\nWe close with Kashif and Meli'sa Morgan — older, a little less hurried. Same idea as where we started.\n\n— Simone",
  "quote": "Some producers make music, some producers make introductions. Norman Connors was the second kind — a jazz drummer who heard what two voices could do together before either of them did.",
  "home_quote": {
    "text": "Thirty years of harmonies lived in, adjusted, *made permanent.*",
    "context": "On The Dells, Love On"
  },
